From 3da7bdc7fb8b0ab4476a2b9ff905efe7e11e6927 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: salvamalfa <salvamalfas@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 04:37:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/19] Extend the margin panel to 17 months and verify a
 second domestic crossing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Rebuilds both AFAC marginals from every origin-destination workbook that
could be retrieved: 2024M01-2025M03 and 2026M01-2026M02, 10,051 route rows
against 143 carrier rows.

The reconciliation is far stronger than the single quarter suggested.
Fifteen of the seventeen months agree EXACTLY, to the passenger. The two
that differ are off by 10 and 36 on totals near five million, consistent
with one publication being restated after the other. Two independently
produced aggregates do not land on the same integer fifteen times unless
both are cut from one table already carrying route and carrier, which is
the argument the transparency request now makes.

Widening the panel also surfaced a second verified domestic crossing. The
2024 workbook carries a lowercase city "Los Cabos", distinct from "SAN JOSÉ
DEL CABO": 6,268 passengers, only to Mazatlán and Culiacán, gone after July
2024. Its monthly figures match Aéreo Calafia's published national total
exactly in five of seven active months, and CIUDAD JUAREZ-CULIACAN closes
May exactly at 32. So "Los Cabos" is Cabo San Lucas and those routes are
entirely Calafia. Eighteen January passengers remain unassigned; no
hypothesis was forced to close them.

Also documents a trap the wider panel exposed: a seed covering only part of
a carrier's network still converges and still honours every route total, but
the split is meaningless. column_scale is the tell, 0.037 on a three-route
seed against nationwide carrier totals, and there is now a test pinning it.

Crosswalk grows to 58 cities, each verified against dim_airport and checked
for code collisions.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
